D={
"haryanka-dynasty":("Bimbisara (r. c. 544–492 BCE) built Magadha's power by marriage alliances with Kosala, the Licchavis and Madra, and by annexing Anga, which gave him the port of Champa and Ganga trade. He organised officials to collect land revenue directly. His son Ajatashatru fought a long war against the Vajji confederacy, reportedly using new weapons such as a catapult and a scythed chariot, and fortified the village of Pataligrama, later Pataliputra.","Haryanka kings are remembered in Buddhist texts as patricides: Ajatashatru is said to have killed Bimbisara, and his successors killed their fathers in turn. Disgusted, the people and ministers deposed the last Haryanka king, Nagadasaka, and raised the minister Shishunaga to the throne around 413 BCE."),
"nanda-empire":("Mahapadma Nanda, said by Puranas to be the son of a Shudra woman, overthrew the Shishunagas and defeated many older kshatriya dynasties, earning the title 'destroyer of all kshatriyas'. The Nandas built the first huge standing army in India — Greek sources speak of 200,000 infantry, 20,000 cavalry, 2,000 chariots and 3,000–6,000 elephants — which deterred Alexander's tired troops from crossing the Beas in 326 BCE.","Heavy taxation and the low-born status ascribed to them made the Nandas unpopular. Tradition says the Brahmin Chanakya, insulted at Dhana Nanda's court, trained the young Chandragupta Maurya, who raised an army in the northwest and overthrew Dhana Nanda around 322 BCE."),
"shunga-dynasty":("In about 185 BCE the commander-in-chief Pushyamitra Shunga assassinated the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, during a military parade and took the throne. He performed Vedic horse sacrifices (ashvamedha) to proclaim sovereignty and fought off Indo-Greek raids; his grandson Vasumitra defeated Greeks on the Sindhu river.","The Shunga realm shrank to Magadha and central India (Vidisha). Its last king Devabhuti was killed in a palace plot by his minister Vasudeva Kanva around 73 BCE, who founded the short-lived Kanva dynasty. Shunga-era art survives on the Bharhut stupa and the Sanchi gateways' early phases."),
"indo-greek-kingdoms":("Greek kingdoms of Bactria, left over from Alexander's conquest and the Seleucid empire, crossed the Hindu Kush around 200 BCE under Demetrius I as Mauryan power faded. Menander I (Milinda, r. c. 165–130 BCE) ruled from Sagala (Sialkot) and is remembered in the Buddhist Milindapanha dialogue with the monk Nagasena.","The Indo-Greeks fragmented into some 30 rival kings and were squeezed by Scythians (Sakas), Parthians and finally the Kushans; the last ruler, Strato II, disappears around 10 CE. They left bilingual Greek–Kharosthi coins with realistic portraits and contributed to Gandhara art."),
"western-kshatrapas":("Saka (Scythian) governors who had served the Kushans established themselves in Gujarat, Malwa and Saurashtra in the 1st century CE. Rudradaman I (r. c. 130–150) defeated the Satavahanas twice and left the Junagadh rock inscription (150 CE) — the earliest long inscription in Sanskrit — recording repairs to the Mauryan Sudarshana lake.","Gupta emperor Chandragupta II conquered them around 395–415 CE, gaining the rich ports of western India; legend links this victory to the hero Vikramaditya. Their dated coins are a key source for Indian chronology."),
"vakataka-dynasty":("Founded by Vindhyashakti around 250 CE in the Deccan after the Satavahanas collapsed, the Vakatakas performed Vedic sacrifices and ruled central India. Chandragupta II married his daughter Prabhavatigupta to the Vakataka king; as regent she ruled for years, binding the two dynasties.","The kingdom split into two branches, which declined in the late 5th century under pressure from neighbours such as the Vishnukundins and Kalachuris. Their great legacy is the later Ajanta caves, sponsored under King Harishena (r. c. 460–478)."),
"pushyabhuti-vardhana-dynasty":("The Pushyabhutis ruled Thanesar (Haryana) and rose by fighting the Hunas. Harshavardhana (r. 606–647) took over Kannauj after his sister's husband was killed, uniting most of north India. Xuanzang described his huge assemblies at Prayag, where Harsha gave away all his wealth every five years.","Harsha was defeated by Pulakeshin II on the Narmada (c. 618) and left no heir; after his death in 647 a minister usurped the throne and the empire disintegrated. Kannauj became the prize fought over by Pratiharas, Palas and Rashtrakutas."),
"gurjara-pratihara-dynasty":("Nagabhata I (c. 730–760) won fame by repelling Arab armies from Sindh, protecting western India. His successors took Kannauj (c. 816) and under Mihira Bhoja (r. 836–885) ruled from Gujarat to Bengal; the Arab traveller Sulaiman called them hostile to Islam but praised their security.","A century of tripartite struggle with Palas and Rashtrakutas exhausted them; Rashtrakuta Indra III sacked Kannauj in 916. Mahmud of Ghazni's raid in 1018 broke them, and smaller Rajput clans (Chandela, Paramara, Chauhan) succeeded."),
"western-chalukyas-kalyani":("Tailapa II, a Rashtrakuta feudatory claiming Chalukya descent, overthrew his overlords in 973 and ruled from Kalyani. His dynasty fought long wars with the Cholas over the Raichur doab. Vikramaditya VI (r. 1076–1126) started his own era and patronised the poet Bilhana and jurist Vijnaneshwara, author of the Mitakshara law commentary.","Feudatories grew powerful — the Kalachuris seized Kalyani in 1157, and Hoysalas, Yadavas and Kakatiyas divided the realm by 1189. Their ornate temples in Lakkundi and Itagi developed the Kalyani Chalukya style."),
"hoysala-empire":("Hill chiefs from the Malnad region of Karnataka, the Hoysalas served as Chalukya feudatories and became independent under Vishnuvardhana (r. 1108–1152), who reportedly converted from Jainism to Vaishnavism under Ramanuja.","They were caught between Pandyas and Yadavas, and the Delhi Sultanate's general Malik Kafur sacked their capital Dwarasamudra in 1311. Veera Ballala III died fighting the Madurai Sultanate in 1343; his officers Harihara and Bukka helped found Vijayanagara. Their star-shaped temples at Belur, Halebidu and Somanathapura are UNESCO-listed."),
"kakatiya-dynasty":("Kakatiyas were Chalukya feudatories in Telangana who became independent under Prola II and Rudra I (c. 1163). Ganapati (r. 1199–1262) and his daughter Rudramadevi, one of India's few reigning queens, built Warangal fort and the Thousand Pillar and Ramappa temples, and an irrigation system of tanks.","The Delhi Sultanate attacked repeatedly; Muhammad bin Tughlaq (then Ulugh Khan) captured Warangal in 1323 and took Prataparudra prisoner. The Kakatiya tanks and Ramappa temple (UNESCO 2021) remain."),
"eastern-ganga-dynasty":("Anantavarman Chodaganga (r. 1078–1147) ruled from the Godavari to the Ganga and began the Jagannath temple at Puri. Narasimhadeva I (r. 1238–1264) defeated Bengal's Muslim rulers and built the Sun Temple at Konark, conceived as a giant stone chariot with 24 wheels.","Weakened by Delhi and Bengal sultanates and internal revolts, the last Ganga king was overthrown in 1434 by his minister Kapilendra Deva, founder of the Gajapati dynasty."),
"mamluk-slave-dynasty":("Qutb ud-Din Aibak, a Turkic slave who rose as Muhammad of Ghor's general, ruled from Delhi from 1206. Iltutmish (r. 1211–1236) consolidated the Sultanate, received caliphal recognition and introduced silver tanka and copper jital coins; his daughter Razia (r. 1236–40) was the only woman to rule Delhi. Balban (r. 1266–87) held off the Mongols with harsh rule.","After Balban's death his weak grandson Kaiqubad was killed, and the Khalji nobles took power in 1290."),
"khalji-dynasty":("Jalaluddin Khalji seized power in 1290, ending the Turkish nobles' monopoly. His nephew Alauddin murdered him in 1296 and, with the loot of Devagiri, made himself sultan. Alauddin defeated several Mongol invasions, introduced market price controls, and sent Malik Kafur on raids to Warangal, Dwarasamudra and Madurai.","After Alauddin's death in 1316, Malik Kafur's brief regency and the murders of rival princes led to the rise of Khusrau Khan, killed by Ghazi Malik (Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq) in 1320."),
"tughlaq-dynasty":("Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq founded the dynasty in 1320 and built Tughlaqabad. Muhammad bin Tughlaq (r. 1325–51) was brilliant but erratic: token currency, a move of capital to Daulatabad and a planned conquest of Khurasan all failed. Firuz Shah (r. 1351–88) built canals, towns and hospitals.","Revolts produced the Bahmani Sultanate and Vijayanagara (1336–47). Timur sacked Delhi in 1398, killing tens of thousands; the dynasty limped on until 1414."),
"lodi-dynasty":("Bahlul Lodi, an Afghan governor, took the throne in 1451. Sikandar Lodi (r. 1489–1517) founded Agra and made it his capital.","Ibrahim Lodi alienated Afghan nobles, who invited Babur from Kabul. Babur's field guns and tactics defeated him at the First Battle of Panipat (1526)."),
"bahmani-sultanate":("Founded in 1347 by Alauddin Bahman Shah, who rebelled against Muhammad bin Tughlaq, with capitals at Gulbarga and Bidar. Minister Mahmud Gawan reformed administration and built a madrasa in Bidar.","Factional conflict between Deccani and foreign nobles, and Gawan's execution in 1481, led to five successor sultanates: Bijapur, Golconda, Ahmadnagar, Berar and Bidar."),
"suri-dynasty":("Sher Shah Suri, an Afghan commander, defeated the Mughal Humayun at Chausa (1539) and Kannauj (1540) and ruled north India. He introduced the silver rupiya, land measurement, and rebuilt the Grand Trunk Road with caravanserais.","Sher Shah died in a gunpowder explosion in 1545. Succession quarrels let Humayun return; Akbar's general defeated Hemu at the Second Battle of Panipat (1556)."),
"rajput-kingdoms-of-mewar-sisodia":("Hammir Singh recaptured Chittor around 1326 after Alauddin Khalji's siege of 1303. Rana Kumbha (r. 1433–68) built the Vijay Stambh and Kumbhalgarh fort; Rana Sanga united Rajputs against Babur.","Mewar never fully submitted: Maharana Pratap fought Akbar at Haldighati (1576). It accepted Mughal suzerainty in 1615 and British protection in 1818, and merged into India in 1948."),
"nizams-of-hyderabad-asaf-jahi":("Asaf Jah I, the Mughal viceroy of the Deccan, became independent in 1724. Later Nizams allied with the British as a 'subsidiary' state.","The last Nizam, Osman Ali Khan, one of the world's richest men, refused to join India; Operation Polo (1948) integrated Hyderabad."),
"seleucid-dynasty":("Seleucus I, Alexander's general, took Babylon in 312 BCE and built an empire from Anatolia to Bactria; he traded India's eastern provinces to Chandragupta for 500 elephants. Antioch became a great city.","Parthians and Bactrians broke away; Rome defeated Antiochus III at Magnesia (190 BCE). Pompey annexed Syria in 63 BCE."),
"parthian-empire":("Arsaces I led the Parni nomads to seize Parthia around 247 BCE; Mithridates I conquered Mesopotamia. Parthian horse archers destroyed a Roman army at Carrhae (53 BCE).","Repeated Roman invasions and civil wars weakened them; their vassal Ardashir I of Persis killed the last king in 224 CE."),
"sasanian-empire":("Ardashir I founded the empire in 224, promoting Zoroastrianism as state religion. Shapur I captured the Roman emperor Valerian (260). Khosrow I (r. 531–579) reformed taxes and welcomed scholars.","An exhausting war with Byzantium (602–628) and civil war left them weak against the Arab armies, which won at Qadisiyya (636) and Nahavand (642)."),
"rashidun-caliphate":("After Muhammad's death in 632, Abu Bakr was chosen caliph and reunited Arabia. Umar's armies conquered Syria, Egypt, Iraq and Persia.","Uthman was killed in 656; the first civil war between Ali and Muawiya followed, and Ali was murdered in 661."),
"umayyad-caliphate":("Muawiya founded the Umayyad dynasty in Damascus. Its armies reached Spain (711) and Sindh (712). Abd al-Malik built the Dome of the Rock and made Arabic the administrative language.","Non-Arab Muslims resented their status; the Abbasid revolution defeated them on the Zab in 750. An Umayyad prince founded the Emirate of Córdoba."),
"abbasid-caliphate":("Founded on the Abbasid revolution in 750; al-Mansur founded Baghdad in 762. Under Harun al-Rashid and al-Mamun the House of Wisdom translated Greek and Indian works.","Provincial dynasties split away, Turkish soldiers took control, and the Mongols under Hulagu sacked Baghdad in 1258."),
"fatimid-caliphate":("Ismaili Shia leader al-Mahdi founded the caliphate in Tunisia (909); they conquered Egypt in 969 and founded Cairo and al-Azhar.","Crusades, famine and military feuds; Saladin abolished it in 1171."),
"seljuk-empire":("Tughril Beg led Oghuz Turks to take Baghdad in 1055. Alp Arslan defeated Byzantium at Manzikert (1071), opening Anatolia to Turks; vizier Nizam al-Mulk founded madrasas.","It fragmented after Malik Shah's death (1092); the Khwarazmians defeated the last Great Seljuk in 1194."),
"ayyubid-dynasty":("Saladin, a Kurdish general, ended the Fatimids, united Egypt and Syria, and recaptured Jerusalem in 1187.","Family rivalries; Mamluk soldiers killed the last sultan in 1250."),
"mamluk-sultanate-egypt":("Slave soldiers seized power in 1250 and defeated the Mongols at Ain Jalut (1260).","The Ottomans conquered Egypt in 1517."),
"ilkhanate":("Hulagu Khan conquered Persia and sacked Baghdad (1258).","Without an heir after 1335 it broke apart."),
"timurid-empire":("Timur conquered from Delhi to Anatolia; his heirs made Samarkand and Herat cultural centres.","Divided among heirs; Uzbeks took Samarkand around 1507."),
"qajar-dynasty":("Agha Mohammad Khan reunited Iran and founded Tehran as capital.","Constitutional revolution (1906); Reza Khan deposed them in 1925."),
"hephthalites-white-huns":("Central Asian nomads who defeated the Sasanians and raided Gupta India (Toramana, Mihirakula).","Defeated by Sasanians and Turks around 560."),
"g-kt-rk-khaganate":("Bumin Qaghan revolted against the Rouran (552). The Orkhon inscriptions are the earliest written Turkic.","Divided into east and west, then destroyed by the Uyghurs in 744."),
"sui-dynasty":("Yang Jian (Emperor Wen) reunified China in 589 and restored examinations.","Emperor Yang's Grand Canal and failed Korean wars caused revolts; Tang took over in 618."),
"yamato-and-imperial-japan":("The Yamato kingdom unified Japan in the 3rd–6th centuries; the emperor line is the world's oldest hereditary monarchy.","Not fallen; the 1947 constitution made the emperor a symbol of the state."),
"kamakura-shogunate":("Minamoto no Yoritomo became shogun after the Genpei War (1185).","Mongol invasions strained it; Emperor Go-Daigo overthrew it in 1333."),
"goryeo":("Wang Geon reunified Korea in 918; Goryeo printed the Tripitaka Koreana and the first metal movable type (Jikji, 1377).","Mongol domination and Yi Seong-gye's coup (1392)."),
"pagan-kingdom":("Anawrahta united the Irrawaddy valley in 1044 and adopted Theravada Buddhism; over 10,000 temples were built.","Mongol invasions (1287)."),
"ayutthaya-kingdom":("Founded by Uthong in 1351; a major trading port.","Burmese sack (1767)."),
"i-vi-t-l-and-tr-n":("Lý Thái Tổ moved the capital to Thăng Long (Hanoi) in 1010; the Trần repelled three Mongol invasions.","Overthrown by Hồ Quý Ly (1400)."),
"malacca-sultanate":("Prince Parameswara founded Malacca around 1400; it became the region's leading port.","Portuguese conquest (1511)."),
"julio-claudian-dynasty":("Augustus founded the Principate after Actium (31 BCE).","Nero's suicide in 68 CE caused civil war."),
"constantinian-dynasty":("Constantine won at the Milvian Bridge (312), legalised Christianity and founded Constantinople.","Julian died in Persia in 363."),
"carolingian-dynasty":("Pepin deposed the Merovingians (751); Charlemagne was crowned emperor (800).","Divided at Verdun (843); Hugh Capet took over in 987."),
"house-of-wessex-and-anglo-saxon-england":("Alfred the Great resisted the Danes and his heirs united England.","The Norman Conquest (1066)."),
"normans-and-plantagenets":("William conquered England in 1066; Magna Carta came in 1215.","Wars of the Roses; Henry Tudor won at Bosworth (1485)."),
"tudor-dynasty":("Henry VII won at Bosworth; Henry VIII broke with Rome; Elizabeth I defeated the Armada.","Elizabeth died childless (1603)."),
"habsburg-dynasty":("Rudolf I was elected in 1273; marriages gained Spain, the Netherlands, Bohemia and Hungary.","Defeat in World War I (1918)."),
"bourbon-dynasty-france":("Henry IV ended the Wars of Religion; Louis XIV built absolutism at Versailles.","The French Revolution (1789–92)."),
"romanov-dynasty":("Michael I was elected in 1613 after the Time of Troubles.","The 1917 revolution; the family was executed in 1918."),
"hohenzollern-prussia-and-germany":("Frederick the Great made Prussia a power; Bismarck united Germany (1871).","Defeat in 1918."),
"macedonian-dynasty-byzantine":("Basil I founded it; Basil II reconquered Bulgaria.","Succession weakness; Manzikert came in 1071."),
"palaiologos-dynasty":("Michael VIII recaptured Constantinople (1261).","The Ottomans took it in 1453."),
"rurikid-dynasty":("Rurik's line ruled Kievan Rus and then Muscovy; Ivan IV was the first tsar.","Fyodor I died without heirs (1598)."),
"piast-and-jagiellonian-poland":("Mieszko I's baptism (966); union with Lithuania (1386).","Ended with the Jagiellonian line in 1572."),
"old-kingdom-of-egypt":("Djoser's step pyramid; Giza pyramids.","Drought and weakened central power."),
"new-kingdom-of-egypt":("Ahmose I expelled the Hyksos; Thutmose III and Ramesses II expanded the empire.","Bronze Age collapse and the power of priests."),
"ptolemaic-dynasty":("Ptolemy I took Egypt after Alexander; the Library of Alexandria flourished.","Cleopatra's defeat at Actium; Rome annexed Egypt (30 BCE)."),
"solomonic-dynasty-ethiopia":("Yekuno Amlak claimed descent from Solomon and the Queen of Sheba.","Haile Selassie was deposed in 1974."),
"zulu-kingdom":("Shaka built a powerful military state.","British victory in the Anglo-Zulu War (1879)."),
"ashanti-empire":("Osei Tutu and the priest Okomfo Anokye united the Asante with the Golden Stool.","British wars; annexed 1901."),
}
